<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Utu</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Shamash</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Utu, called Shamash in Akkadian, was the Mesopotamian god of the sun, justice and truth. Each day he crossed the sky and saw all deeds, so he was invoked as judge and as protector of travellers; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a>'s law code shows him handing the king authority. His chief temples, both named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/e-babbar">E-babbar</a>, were at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larsa">Larsa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sippar">Sippar</a>, and he was also worshipped in later Hatra and elsewhere in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/utu.jpg" alt="Utu" width="320" height="484" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Katolophyromai · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tablet_of_Shamash_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After this first city declined, a second city flourished from about 2550 to 2300 BCE, in the Early Dynastic period, with a royal palace and temples to Ishtar, <b>Shamash</b> and other gods.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mari">Mari · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Among the greatest were Enlil, king of the gods, An (Akkadian Anu), Enki (Ea), the moon god Nanna (Sîn), the sun god Utu (<b>Shamash</b>) and Inanna, goddess of sex and war.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mesopotamian-religion">Mesopotamian religion · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>At its top a relief appears to show Hammurabi standing before the seated <b>Shamash</b>, the Babylonian sun god and god of justice, who wears the horned crown of divinity with flames spouting from his shoulders.</blockquote><a href="/work/code-of-hammurabi">Code of Hammurabi · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>At Sippar about 200 nadītu of the sun god <b>Shamash</b> lived in a walled cloister (gagûm).</blockquote><a href="/societies/old-babylonia">Social Hierarchy of Old Babylonia</a></li><li><blockquote>Nabu-apla-iddina restored the cult of <b>Shamash</b> at Sippar, commemorated on the Sun-god Tablet.</blockquote><a href="/state/dynasty-of-e">Dynasty of E</a></li><li><blockquote>At its centre stood a vast temple precinct to the sun god <b>Shamash</b>, called Maran ('our lord'), with shrines to Allat, Baalshamin and other deities.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-hatra">Kingdom of Hatra</a></li></ul></section>
